WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.535
Picture yourself in the
following scenario.

2
00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:05.865
You're managing the database
of a large online store.

3
00:00:05.865 --> 00:00:07.380
Your database must be able to

4
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.910
retrieve the customer's
details from

5
00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.530
one table and then find

6
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.915
the order recorded
against another table.

7
00:00:12.915 --> 00:00:14.985
How does the database establish

8
00:00:14.985 --> 00:00:17.790
a relationship between
these pieces of data?

9
00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.410
Over the next few minutes,

10
00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.515
you'll explore this process
and by the end of this video,

11
00:00:22.515 --> 00:00:23.940
you'll be able to explain

12
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.650
why data in a database should be

13
00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.795
related and identify an instance

14
00:00:27.795 --> 00:00:30.165
of related data in a database.

15
00:00:30.165 --> 00:00:34.500
Data stored in a database
cannot exist in isolation.

16
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.115
It must have a relationship
with other data

17
00:00:37.115 --> 00:00:40.400
so that it can be processed
into meaningful information.

18
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.620
How do you make sure
that all the data

19
00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.005
in your database is related?

20
00:00:45.005 --> 00:00:47.390
Let's explore how
data is related by

21
00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:50.165
using the online
store as our example.

22
00:00:50.165 --> 00:00:52.715
In the database of
your online store,

23
00:00:52.715 --> 00:00:55.660
you could have an order
table and a customer table.

24
00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.325
To locate the details
of a customer's order,

25
00:00:58.325 --> 00:01:01.460
you will check the order number
against the customer ID.

26
00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.890
In other words, the
database establishes

27
00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:06.530
a link between the
data in the tables.

28
00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:09.460
Let's look at the customer
table in more detail.

29
00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:13.140
In this table, the
columns are Customer ID,

30
00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.335
FirstName, LastName, and Email.

31
00:01:16.335 --> 00:01:19.985
In relational database
terms, these are fields.

32
00:01:19.985 --> 00:01:21.920
Then there are
several rows which

33
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.370
contain data for each
of these fields.

34
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:26.420
In relational databases, they're

35
00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.805
known as records of the table.

36
00:01:28.805 --> 00:01:31.220
All these fields and rows work

37
00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:33.455
together to store
information on the customer,

38
00:01:33.455 --> 00:01:35.540
also known as the entity.

39
00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.120
Every row and record
in the customer table

40
00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.360
is an instance of
the customer entity.

41
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.560
For example, Sarah Hogan,

42
00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:44.960
who is a customer ID of C1,

43
00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:48.945
is one customer instance
and Katrina Langley,

44
00:01:48.945 --> 00:01:53.195
who is a customer ID of C4 is
another customer instance.

45
00:01:53.195 --> 00:01:55.340
What's most important
is that each of

46
00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:56.780
these customer instances or

47
00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.900
records must be
uniquely identifiable.

48
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:03.185
But what if two or more
customers share similar info,

49
00:02:03.185 --> 00:02:06.050
like the same first
name or last name.

50
00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:08.975
To avoid this confusion
within the database,

51
00:02:08.975 --> 00:02:11.075
you can use a field
that contains

52
00:02:11.075 --> 00:02:13.735
only unique values
like the customer ID.

53
00:02:13.735 --> 00:02:16.560
This is called a
primary key field.

54
00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.740
It contains unique
values that cannot

55
00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.155
be replicated elsewhere
in the table.

56
00:02:21.155 --> 00:02:23.930
Even if two customers
share the same name,

57
00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:26.700
they'll still have
separate customer IDs.

58
00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.730
This means that
your database can

59
00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.285
determine which customer
is the required one.

60
00:02:31.285 --> 00:02:33.990
Let's look at the
order table next.

61
00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:35.960
Just like the customer table,

62
00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.890
the order table also
has fields and records.

63
00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:42.685
In this table, the primary
key field is the order ID.

64
00:02:42.685 --> 00:02:45.545
But there's also a
field named customer ID

65
00:02:45.545 --> 00:02:48.725
with the exact same data
as the customer data.

66
00:02:48.725 --> 00:02:52.030
What is the purpose of the
customer ID in this table?

67
00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:54.080
The Customer ID is there to help

68
00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.735
identify who it is
that place the order.

69
00:02:56.735 --> 00:02:59.840
By adding the customer ID
field to the order table,

70
00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.790
our relationship is
established between

71
00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:04.270
the customer table
and the order table.

72
00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.065
Because of this relationship,

73
00:03:06.065 --> 00:03:09.755
you can pull data in a
meaningful way from both tables.

74
00:03:09.755 --> 00:03:11.780
The customer ID field in

75
00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.815
the order table is known
as the foreign key field.

76
00:03:14.815 --> 00:03:17.780
A foreign key is a
field in one table that

77
00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.645
connects to the primary key
field in the original table,

78
00:03:20.645 --> 00:03:23.330
which in this case is
the customer table.

79
00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:26.945
The customer ID is the primary
key of the customer table,

80
00:03:26.945 --> 00:03:30.050
but it becomes a foreign
key in the order table.

81
00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:32.705
This way, the relationship
is established

82
00:03:32.705 --> 00:03:35.480
and the data in these
two tables are related.

83
00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.190
You should now be
able to explain

84
00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:38.750
the relationships
between data in

85
00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:40.550
a database and identify

86
00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:44.070
instances of related
data. Great work.